hey everyone if you're new to this  channel my name is lucy and i make  videos all about tech  career and student advice so if that  sounds like something you're interested  in  please consider subscribing to stay  updated so as you can probably tell from  the title of this video  i did fail my aws sysops administrator  associate exam  not once but twice and honestly it's  something that's a bit embarrassing for  me  considering i passed three aws exams  beforehand on the first try  but i think it's something that is quite  important for me to  share with all of you because there were  so many learnings that came out of it  so many things that i wish i could have  done differently and going forward i  think there will be a lot of mistakes  that i'll end up avoiding in the future  so i'm going to be breaking up this  video into three parts  i want to talk about the real reasons  why i failed twice my advice to people  who want to take an aws exam  and what resources i used for the sysops  administrator certification  if you're interested in seeing a  particular section of this video i've  included time stamps in the description  below  so feel free to skip around to what  you'll find the most useful  let's get into it okay so now i'm going  to talk about the real reasons i failed  the exam twice  i think the biggest reason is because i  really underestimated the difficulty of  the exam  a bit about my cloud learning journey i  started off in august  i did the certified cloud practitioner  certification that took about three  weeks  i then did the aws solutions architect  associate  which took another month and then the  month after i sat the aws  developer associate exam so before  sitting the aws sysops exam the exam  that i failed  i achieved three certification exams in  three months  and at that time i was feeling quite  confident and i thought that the  aws associate would just be the fourth  one that i passed  a month later so because of that i  wanted to  challenge myself a bit further and set  the exam a bit earlier  by sitting it two weeks after the  developer associate  and what i was studying for the exam  i really took it slow um i didn't really  study much the first week i wanted to  take a bit of a break  so that only left me one week to study  for the sisops administrative exam  which i think is definitely possible to  pass  in that in a short amount of time for  certain people  but for me i don't come from a very  technical background i don't really have  too much hands-on experience with aws  and i know that there's a lot of overlap  between the three  exams but even still i think it was  quite a bit of a stretch  to put the exam that close right after  the  developer associate exam so i guess the  main thing was  feeling a bit too overconfident and  thinking the exam was easier than it  actually was  especially after looking at some forums  online and seeing people talk about how  the sysops exam  has a lot of content that is already  covered in the previous three  certifications i thought that it would  be okay to skim through the video  lectures for the concepts that  i've already seen before but what i've  realized is that each exam  approaches the aws services in a  different set of lens  so for example the sysops administrator  exam it really looks at these  concepts through the lens of someone  who's a systops or devops  engineer working with aws another reason  i failed twice  is because i didn't really take the best  approach when studying for this exam  if you've seen my previous video on how  i passed through aws certifications in  three months  you'd have seen that i emphasized a lot  on doing hands-on lab exercises  and because i gave myself only one week  to study for the sysops administrator  exam  i didn't really do too many lab  exercises  i focused more on doing practice exams  and these practice exams  after a while i found myself just  memorizing these answers or  seeing a question and not already  knowing the answer because i've done  the same practice exam material over and  over again and this is not the best  approach because when you go into the  exam you get a completely different set  of questions and they'll have  way different answers because of the way  that the questions are phrased  so it's not the best approach to just  keep doing practice exams without really  knowing  deep down what's underneath these  services and  that was a big mistake that i made  because i think that not only  made me fail the exam but it also  stunted my learning as well  so i made sure that after fame twice the  third time  i went back to the video content and i  did all the lab exercises that they had  and i think that really helps because  for the sysops exam  more so than the other ones hands-on ex  hands-on experience is very necessary  because a big part of the exam is about  troubleshooting so the questions might  be something like  okay so you have this service this  service goes down  what could be the potential reasons and  if you don't have  so if you have the aws systems exam  coming up or  just for any other awc exam in general  please remember to do  hands-on exercises to make sure that  you're able to validate your knowledge  and it's better for memory retention as  well even after  you finish your exam it's easier for you  to  keep the knowledge fresh in your mind so  that in the future if you ever need it  for your role it's way easier for you to  pick the skills back  up so moving on to the second part of  this video i wanted to share some exam  tips  on how to pass aways exams in general  but also how to pass the sysops  administrator exam  on this board you can see there's a  cycle of learning  to start off with i recommend learning  the content that is relevant to the aws  exam that you're going to be taking  and when you feel ready applying what  you've learned to hands-on lab exercises  and then following that up with practice  exams it's okay at the start if you end  up getting 50  60 or even 40 in your practice exams i  think practice exams are a really good  way to tell  where your gaps are in your knowledge so  that you can go back to the learning  material  and that way you can make sure that you  not only pass the real exam but you  understand  everything that is required for you to  know in each aws certification  so in terms of advice more specifically  for the syslops exam  i would really recommend diving into the  concepts of monitoring and  troubleshooting in a lot of practice  exams i've seen that there's a very big  emphasis on the cloud watch  whether that be cloudwatch events  cloudless alarms car watch logs and it's  very important for you to know the  difference between these services  and when you should be applying it to  which scenario with the aws sysops  administrator exam  you need to know a lot more than what  these services are what they do  but also on how you can use these  services to  create fault tolerant highly available  applications something that i found very  useful  is reading faqs of each of the aws  services a lot of services like ec2  cloudwatch s3  rds they have their own sets of faqs and  by reading these documents you'll be  able to gain a more holistic  understanding of what you might get  tested on for the final part of this  video i wanted to talk about what  resources i used for the sysops  administrator exam  and what i would recommend doing so the  resources that  i've used is already linked in the  description below so i'm not going to  spend too long on this section because i  think it really depends on  the person what your preferred learning  style is whether you  like ebooks or like video courses it's  okay if you want to use half of one  course before realizing that you prefer  the teaching style of another course  try things out and see what you like and  what's best for you  and this wraps up my video of how i  failed an aws exam  twice i hope you got something out of  this video please give it a like if you  did  and subscribe for more tech student  advice and career related videos
 

